<!--CLI Documentation — TargetBootstrap
  FQN: com.machineblocks.bml.cli.documentation.TargetBootstrap-->
<clidoc:TargetBootstrap xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:clidoc="com.machineblocks.bml.cli.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    How the host registers machineblocks-v3-scad
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Before compile, the host performs the equivalent of:

        import { registerTarget } from "@blockml/compiler";
        import { BlockRenderEngine } from "@blockml/block-render-engine";
        import { MachineBlocksRenderTarget } from "@machineblocks/render-target";

        registerTarget(new MachineBlocksRenderTarget(new BlockRenderEngine()));

    The target name property is the string machineblocks-v3-scad.
    Registration is idempotent for the process.

    The universal CLI's bootstrapTargets loads blockdoc, xsd, and fatblock only.
    It throws on an unknown name in targets[]. This host must not add
    machineblocks-v3-scad to that built-in list inside @blockml/cli. It calls
    registerTarget directly (or registerTargetFactory from the host API).

    validate and inspect do not register the target.

    Browser callers and tests that use the render target without this CLI call
    registerTarget themselves with the same constructor pair.

    CompileOptions passed into the compiler set outputBasePath to the real path
    of srcDir. targetOptions["machineblocks-v3-scad"] carries mbLibPath,
    settingsFormat when set, and, for an explicit mapping, sourcePackage and
    targetPackage. Automatic mapping omits those two keys. activeConfig is
    unused by compile.

  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="clidoc:Installation" relation="ReadNext" />
  </references>
</clidoc:TargetBootstrap>
